**Date:** 30 October 2026

**Supersedes:** [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject).
That accepted record is preserved unchanged, not rewritten to match this review.

## Context

The [review of #4471](/kb/incidents/4471-split) separates a shared-parser problem
from a booking-v3 date-hint problem. Similar subjects hid different causes;
different subjects described the same cause. Browser-derived intake tags also
disagreed with the tenant and entry-path evidence.

The old decision's stated trade-off covered splitting reports of the same bug
across different subjects. It did not justify merging unrelated causes under
one subject and leaving a problem without its own engineering issue.

## Decision

Treat reports as duplicates only when they concern the same product and the
evidence supports the same underlying cause. Read the body and resolve the
tenant's locale, booking form, and report channel before deciding.

For the exported `sched-prod` date reports, reuse `tenantOf`, `causeOf`, and
`sameCause` in `src/lib/kb`. Locale order comes from `Intl.DateTimeFormat`;
intake tags and browser locales are not authoritative. Missing tenant context
produces an unknown cause and must not become an automatic match.

Count distinct report IDs, not overlapping export rows. Keep contradictory
observations visible and seek more evidence before inventing another cause.

## Executable examples

<DedupeExplorer />

The same tenant can have different causes through the form and app. Reports
with different subjects can share a cause. The example deliberately refuses
to merge reports from an unknown tenant even if their subjects are identical.

## Consequences

Triage requires reading and context lookup instead of a subject-only shortcut.
Engineering gets separate issues for independently fixable mechanisms, with
counts grounded in the export. A fix for one mechanism cannot close the other
without evidence.

The [triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe) and
[original incident page](/kb/incidents/4471) now point to this decision and the
separate engineering issues. Re-review incoming desk changes instead of treating
this export as permanently current.

